---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="The OTAKU Manifesto"
  description="Fluid Structure Design: five pillars and three repository shapes that govern every PHPRise project."
  path="/manifesto"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The OTAKU Manifesto</p>
      <h1>Fluid Structure Design</h1>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Canonical edition.</strong> The philosophy now lives independently of
          PHP at <a href="https://otakumanifesto.org">otakumanifesto.org</a>, with the
          full cross-language guide. This page is the PHPRise summary.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        OTAKU reflects a technical obsession with mastery, discipline and structural
        purity. It is not a style guide — it is the set of constraints that decides
        whether a piece of code is allowed to exist.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="wrap grid grid--3">
      <article class="pillar">
        <span class="pillar__letter">O</span>
        <h3>Own your Discipline</h3>
        <p class="card__body">
          Quality is not an accident, it is the result of intentional restriction.
          The nine rules of Object Calisthenics are mandatory: one level of
          indentation per method, no <code>else</code>, wrap every primitive, first
          class collections, one dot per line, no abbreviations, small entities, at
          most two instance variables, no getters or setters.
        </p>
        <p class="pillar__rule">
          Write code that never needs “fixing” because it was built under absolute
          constraint.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="pillar">
        <span class="pillar__letter">T</span>
        <h3>Tools for Composition</h3>
        <p class="card__body">
          Like Unix tools, each component does one thing well. Architecture is a set
          of small, replaceable parts connected by clear inputs and outputs. The
          application flow is a data pipe: input enters through adapters, traverses
          the core, and exits.
        </p>
        <p class="pillar__rule">
          Treat your application flow as a data pipe, keeping logic isolated from side
          effects.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="pillar">
        <span class="pillar__letter">A</span>
        <h3>Armor the Core</h3>
        <p class="card__body">
          Business logic is the most valuable asset and must be protected from
          external change. Dependencies point inwards only. The heart — entities and
          use cases — never knows about frameworks, databases or external APIs.
        </p>
        <p class="pillar__rule">
          The core must never know about frameworks, databases or external APIs.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="pillar">
        <span class="pillar__letter">K</span>
        <h3>Keep Infrastructure Silent</h3>
        <p class="card__body">
          Databases, mailers and brokers are secondary details. Persistence is treated
          as an in-memory collection through the Repository pattern, and entities are
          plain objects that know nothing about how they are stored.
        </p>
        <p class="pillar__rule">
          Your entities are POPOs — completely ignorant of how they are saved or
          transmitted.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="pillar">
        <span class="pillar__letter">U</span>
        <h3>Universal Language &amp; Contracts</h3>
        <p class="card__body">
          Ubiquitous Language bridges business and code, and Design-First Contracts
          make it explicit. The OpenAPI document is the immutable technical promise
          that allows systems to communicate.
        </p>
        <p class="pillar__rule">
          If the code does not read like the business expert speaks, or if the
          contract is bypassed, the system is broken.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="taxonomy">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Repository taxonomy</p>
      <h2>The three forms</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Modularity is enforced at the source. Every repository must fit exactly one
        of these archetypes — there is no fourth option and no hybrid.
      </p>

      <div class="prose mt-2">
        <h3>1. Atomic repositories — the atom</h3>
        <p>
          The smallest possible unit of granularity. If a component can be broken down
          further, it is not an atom. The structure is flat: a single level of
          directory inside <code>src/</code>, fulfilling one specific contract.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          Examples: <code>common-contract</code>, <code>http-value-object</code>,
          <code>http-client</code>.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Assembly repositories — the assembly</h3>
        <p>
          Connection points. They contain no logic; they orchestrate the union of
          atoms through <code>composer.json</code> dependencies, so that one install
          brings ten atoms at once.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          Examples: <code>common</code>, <code>http</code>,
          <code>data-transfer-object</code>.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Application repositories — the core</h3>
        <p>
          The living system. Not a library, but the implementation of the business
          heart, following Clean Architecture and DDD. It stays lean: anything generic
          is pushed down into atoms.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted">
          Examples: <code>ensemble</code>, <code>koenma-id</code>,
          <code>grogu-gateway</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="hero__actions">
        <a class="button" href="/projects">Browse by shape</a>
        <a class="button button--ghost" href="https://github.com/phprise-foundation/otaku-atomic" rel="noopener">Start from a template ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
